WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:03.750
<v ->A solutions architect needs to reduce costs</v>

2
00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.120
for a big data application.

3
00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.850
The application environment consists of hundreds

4
00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.990
of devices that send events to Amazon Kinesis Data Streams.

5
00:00:12.990 --> 00:00:15.690
The device ID is used as the partition key,

6
00:00:15.690 --> 00:00:18.720
so each device gets a separate shard.

7
00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.060
Each device sends between 50 kilobytes

8
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.060
and 450 kilobytes of data per second.

9
00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.270
The shards are pulled

10
00:00:26.144 --> 00:00:27.600
by Lambda function that processes the data

11
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.090
and stores the results on Amazon S3.

12
00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:33.450
Every hour, a Lambda function runs an Amazon Athena

13
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.140
query against the result data that identifies any outliers

14
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.290
and places them in Amazon SQS queue.

15
00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:43.920
An Amazon EC2 Auto Scaling group of two EC2 instances

16
00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:47.520
monitors the queue and runs a short that lasts approximately

17
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.790
30 seconds, process to address the outliers.

18
00:00:50.790 --> 00:00:53.970
The devices submit an average of 10 outlying values,

19
00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:55.380
every hour.

20
00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.840
Which combination of changes to the application

21
00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.910
would most reduce cost?

22
00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:01.770
And we have to select two.

23
00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:04.200
So this is a very, very, very long question

24
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.400
but, I really like it

25
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.500
because it's a complete solution architecture

26
00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:09.570
and there's lots of things we can discuss on it.

27
00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:11.220
So let's draw it out.

28
00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.530
Kinesis will have multiple shards

29
00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:15.900
and have 50 shards in this example.

30
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:19.320
And the question says that we have exactly one chart,

31
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:23.520
per device. So each device that sends us data will get

32
00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:24.840
its own shard.

33
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.110
We also get some information that each device sends

34
00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:31.590
about 50 to 450 kilobytes per second of data,

35
00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.180
into each shard.

36
00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:36.270
Okay? Then we get a Lambda function that reads

37
00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:38.100
from the shards, processes the data,

38
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:41.133
and then writes it into Amazon S3.

39
00:01:41.970 --> 00:01:43.980
Then we have another Lambda function that is

40
00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:46.860
triggered every hour possibly by CloudWatch events

41
00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:48.210
but, it's not mentioned in this question

42
00:01:48.210 --> 00:01:49.713
so I will not put it here.

43
00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:54.720
And so this Lambda function is using Athena to issue a query

44
00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.930
against S3 and it will use the results

45
00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:02.930
of this Athena query to insert into SQS about 10 messages

46
00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.300
per hour, which represents outlying values.

47
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.970
And this SQS queue will be processed

48
00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:12.120
by an Auto Scaling group of two EC2 instances,

49
00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.250
that continuously pull from the queue.

50
00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.530
And whenever they receive a message,

51
00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.260
they do about 30 seconds of processing.

52
00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:21.690
So, if we look at 30 seconds times 10,

53
00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:24.810
this is about, 300 seconds of processing every hour.

54
00:02:24.810 --> 00:02:26.520
So, it is deeply inefficient.

55
00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.940
And so we're asked to find ways to cost optimize

56
00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:31.560
this architecture.

57
00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:33.390
So, we need to choose two options.

58
00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:35.550
And so I'm going to look at all the answers and then

59
00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:36.780
group them together.

60
00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.270
Option A, change the Auto Scaling group

61
00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:41.820
launch configuration to use smaller instance types,

62
00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:43.650
in the same instance family.

63
00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:47.070
B, replace the ASG with a Lambda function triggered

64
00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.950
by messages arriving in the SQS queue.

65
00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:53.970
C, reconfigure the devices and data stream to set ratio

66
00:02:53.970 --> 00:02:58.530
of 10 devices to one data shard stream, stream shard, sorry.

67
00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:01.680
Question option D, reconfigure the devices and data stream

68
00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.420
to set a ratio of two devices to one data stream shard.

69
00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.930
And E, change the desired capacity of the ASG to a single

70
00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:11.640
EC2 instances.

71
00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:15.480
So again, I look at these options and I group them together

72
00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.910
to see which one relate to one another.

73
00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:21.120
So, option group one is to change the ASG.

74
00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.100
This would use smaller size instances,

75
00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:26.280
use limited function or to only use one EC2 instance.

76
00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.410
So, the first option is to have the same architecture,

77
00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:30.660
but we use small instances.

78
00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.510
So, this would definitely give us some cost savings,

79
00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:36.060
we're not sure how much because we don't, not sure how much

80
00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.670
how big, how much bigger the instances were in the past

81
00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.550
but this would definitely give us some cost savings.

82
00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.160
And then obviously using smaller instances

83
00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:47.430
maybe the processing will be longer, who knows?

84
00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.020
But this is something which you take into account.

85
00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.780
We have to optimize for cost right now.

86
00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.960
Number two, is to replace with a Lambda function.

87
00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.000
And this Lambda function will be triggered whenever

88
00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.490
a message arrives in the SQS Queue.

89
00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:03.060
And then finally, we can only use one EC2 instances

90
00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:05.040
instead of two and see how it works.

91
00:04:05.040 --> 00:04:08.370
So, all these solutions are feasible, okay?

92
00:04:08.370 --> 00:04:10.080
Definitely feasible.

93
00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.410
They all go under the time limit, and a Lambda function

94
00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.770
works because Lambda function can have a maximum time out

95
00:04:16.770 --> 00:04:18.510
of 15 minutes.

96
00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:22.020
And so we are well under with a 30 seconds processing.

97
00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:25.740
So in this example what we have to optimize for is an amount

98
00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.710
of time these EC2 instances will be running

99
00:04:28.710 --> 00:04:30.330
and not doing anything.

100
00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:33.630
So, in the first example remember, we have 300 seconds

101
00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:35.190
of processing per hour.

102
00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.470
And so that means that for the rest of the time

103
00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:41.520
for about 3,300 seconds an hour

104
00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:43.470
the EC2 instances will be idle.

105
00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:46.410
So in the first case and the third case, no matter what

106
00:04:46.410 --> 00:04:50.040
we have unused processing power from our EC2 instances.

107
00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.200
And that is really, really not efficient.

108
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:55.200
So Lambda is the perfect answer to this question

109
00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:57.420
because Lambda will be triggered only when

110
00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:00.060
we receive messages in the SQS queue,

111
00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:02.820
and it will be triggered only 10 times per hour.

112
00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:05.310
So this is perfect, Lambda will give us

113
00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:07.860
the best cost optimization.

114
00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.480
And, option group two.

115
00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:12.630
So we reconfigure the devices for a ratio of 10 devices

116
00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:15.600
to one data stream or for two to one.

117
00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:17.100
So let's have an example.

118
00:05:17.100 --> 00:05:19.680
We have 10 devices into one shard,

119
00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:22.380
or two devices into one shard.

120
00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:26.010
How do we select the one that saves us the most cost?

121
00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:29.070
Well, if we look at the first answer we would have

122
00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:31.980
a lot less shards because we have more devices per shard.

123
00:05:31.980 --> 00:05:34.920
And if we look at the second answer we have less devices

124
00:05:34.920 --> 00:05:37.470
per shard, so we would have less cost saving.

125
00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.630
But are they possible?

126
00:05:39.630 --> 00:05:43.170
So, if we have 10 devices giving into one shard,

127
00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:45.690
that means that because each device generates

128
00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:49.650
between 50 kilobytes and 450 kilobytes of data per second

129
00:05:49.650 --> 00:05:52.440
that means that 10 devices together will generate

130
00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:56.850
between 500 kilobytes and 4,500 kilobytes per second.

131
00:05:56.850 --> 00:05:59.610
Whereas when we have two devices, they will generate

132
00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:03.450
between 100 kilobytes and 900 kilobytes per second.

133
00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:06.420
So, what is possible and what is not possible?

134
00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:09.780
You have to remember for Kinesis data stream that each shard

135
00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:12.780
has a limit of one megabytes per second,

136
00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.450
which represents 1000 kilobytes per second.

137
00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:18.480
If we go over that one megabyte per second limit

138
00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:21.630
we are going to get a throughput exception.

139
00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:25.440
And so, in this case the only feasible solution,

140
00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:28.920
is solution number two because we are going to be

141
00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:31.020
under the one megabyte per second limit.

142
00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:33.390
Unfortunately, solution number one is way

143
00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:35.070
over the one megabytes per second limit,

144
00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:36.510
and so it is infeasible.

145
00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:39.360
Even though it would save more cost for us.

146
00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.090
So in this example, we have to rule out option one,

147
00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:43.740
and we have to go with option D.

148
00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.380
We have to use a ratio of two devices

149
00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:49.320
to one data stream shard, and that is the best practice.

150
00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:53.670
Obviously, for Kinesis to have many devices onto one shard,

151
00:06:53.670 --> 00:06:56.640
as long as you're under the throughput's limits.

152
00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:58.410
So that's it for this question, I hope you liked it

153
00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.360
and I will see you in the next lecture.

